A1. Keeps the eye on the ball. ~ CHAPERONE
A9. These used to rubber neck. ~ BOAS
A10. The original hiker. ~ NOMAD
A11. These hold their liquor well. ~ VATS
A12. This fellow may be no good, but he has an M P at the end of his name. ~ SCAMP
A13. Bread and butter letters. ~ BB
A15. Readin', ritin' and 'rithmatic. ~ LORE
A16. His lie on the green. ~ SCORE
A17. When an aria begins this way, it's bad from start to finish. ~ MAL
A18. What the gent in the gutter said to the cop. ~ HICK
A19. What mom does with pop's trousers these days. ~ WEARS
A20. A possible trick. ~ RUSE
A21. Starting off a pace. ~ APA
A22. Speakeasy. ~ PRATE
A23. Jews, Methodists, Baptists are these. ~ SECTS
A24. The famous R. L. S. in the pi. ~ RSL
A25. You'd know a riot would end this way. ~ IOT
A26. These are made longer by cutting both ends. ~ DITCHES
A28. Iron scrapper (abbr.). ~ USN
A29. Co-producer of the new bawl bearing, scream line model, with changeable seat covers. ~ DAD
A30. This starts from the ground up. ~ SAUSAGE
A34. All dimpled up. ~ COY
A35. When it's slack, this measure carries weight. ~ TON
A37. Many a run on these. ~ HOSES
A38. What a good movie fan does in summer. ~ COOLS
A40. This came after the call to arms. ~ HUG
A41. Fired up. ~ IRED
A42. What in the thunder is this? (plur.). ~ CLAPS
A43. A man got a life sentence for stealing this. ~ KISS
A44. Nowadays these foam at the mouth (abbr.). ~ PTS
A45. Many have kicked the bucket here. ~ DAIRY
A46. Fast work at the table. ~ DIET
A47. This gets the boys (abbr.). ~ SA
A48. The girls hung these on the willow trees when they went near the waters of Babylon. ~ HARPS
A49. Solomon decided to cut in this way to please the ladies. ~ HALF
A50. Gay deceivers. ~ IAGOS
A51. This kind of person is used to high life. ~ TALL
A52. You can take the whole from this and still have some left. ~ WHOLESOME

D1. A coo coo place. ~ COTE
D2. Gets what he gets when he gets it. ~ HAS
D3. Ver. 2 à la cockney. ~ AS
D4. Played the hands. ~ ENCORED
D5. Blows out bellows. ~ ROARS
D6. The finish of the Somme. ~ OMME
D7. Doctors recommend this to make one sleep. ~ NAP
D8. A cut up (abbr.). ~ ED
D9. The dog's meow. ~ BARK
D11. Chords twanged by crooners. ~ VOCAL
D12. Scram, Tommy. ~ SCAT
D13. It's simply pouring water on a duck's back to do this. ~ BASTE
D14. Head work for the parson (plur.). ~ BLESSINGS
D15. These are meant to kiss and make up. ~ LIPS
D16. The set up at the night club table. ~ SEAT
D17. The kind of a do Shakespeare did. ~ MUCH
D18. The toughest ships to conquer in battle. ~ HARDSHIPS
D19. A word never pronounced right. ~ WRONG
D20. Got what he got when he got it (contr.). ~ RECD
D22. This has a leaning for the Fascists (plur.). ~ PISAS
D23. Spare tire holders. ~ STAYS
D27. God-forsaken figures. ~ IDOLS
D28. Well, at any rate, this is still employed. ~ USED
D31. A good lead from hearts. ~ AORTA
D32. What a wise guy does with discretion. ~ USES
D33. Large total in Maine last summer which Republicans and Democrats will long remember. ~ ECLIPSE
D34. A kind of cat. ~ COPY
D35. A fellow feeling in the subway. ~ THIEF
D36. To bounce. ~ OUST
D39. These are always starting rows. ~ OARS
D42. Common to Christmas and Roumania. ~ CAROL
D43. If you do this to your dog in July, he won't go mad in August. ~ KILL
D45. He's always found in the hole. ~ DAGO
D46. Ho hum—another depression. ~ DALE
D48. This makes a noise like a laugh. ~ HAH
D49. After the leg was amputated, this was completely cured. ~ HAM
D50. International whip crackers. ~ IW
D51. A lean too. ~ TO


